3. CASE TEXT AND GROUNDING
--------------------------
AWPTCM T43848 "IPv4_ARP - Proxy ARP".

STEP TEXT: NONE EXISTS.  No exact-title match in ck.db (checked at triage for all 13 campaign
titles; only T43849 has step text).  Reference rows in the same family -- AWP-4353 / 4354 / 4355
Proxy ARP -- exist but are NOT this case and are not presented as its steps.

Grounded in:
  (a) X960 platform support TPS row 23 "Proxy-ARP" -- priority 1, "Yes", note column empty.
  (b) `ip_feature_overview_guide.pdf` (repo root `Feature Overview Guides/`), Proxy ARP, p6.
  (c) The DUT's own CLI, read-only `?` probe, in interface mode:
        awplus(config-if)# ip proxy-arp ?
          <cr>
        awplus(config-if)# ip local-proxy-arp ?
          <cr>
      Neither takes a sub-option.  `ip proxy-arp` is an INTERFACE command, which is what makes
      step 4 below possible.
  (d) STANDING-ORDERS Section 7, the campaign queue's explicit instruction that proxy ARP is an
      ARP-LEVEL question ("a ping would hide the whole mechanism"), and memory
      `satisfactory-level-of-testing`.

OBJECTIVE: with `ip proxy-arp` on an interface, the DUT answers ARP requests arriving on that
interface for addresses it can reach via a DIFFERENT interface, using its own MAC; it does not
answer for addresses it cannot route, nor for addresses on the receiving interface's own subnet;
the behaviour is per-interface; the answered address is genuinely reachable through the DUT; the
command is removable; and it survives a reboot.

**NO PING IS USED ANYWHERE IN THIS CASE.**  A ping would succeed identically with proxy ARP on
or off (the DUT routes between vlan10 and vlan48 either way) and would therefore prove nothing
about the feature.  Every leg is a crafted ARP request, and the measurement is WHO ANSWERED AND
WITH WHICH MAC.


