7. OBSERVATION -- A FOREIGN DEVICE IS LIVE ON THE eth3 SEGMENT  (bench note, did not confound)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
One capture, L3-endtoend-eth3.pcap, holds two frames that are not ours and not the DUT's:

  x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.33 tell 10.38.22.41  [eth 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  x1  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.33 is-at 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e   [eth 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e -> 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53]

Something at 10.38.22.41 with MAC 00:0c:25:2a:a5:53 ARPed for the box's own eth3 address
(10.38.22.33) and the box's kernel answered.  Neither address nor MAC belongs to swi_d
(e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43), to swi_a, to swi_b or to any scapy fiction used in this campaign, and
10.38.22.41 is not in bench-state.md.

It did NOT confound anything in this group: it is in 10.38.22.32/27, whereas every measurement
here is in 192.168.10.0/24 or 192.168.48.0/24, every counted frame is matched by target address
and MAC, and the tool's own frames carry a `CKARPLEG-<tag>` payload marker.  Recorded because a
third party on a supposedly point-to-point bench segment is worth the Test Engineer knowing
about, and because it would matter to any future case that uses the box's real eth3 subnet --
including T33246 in this group, which plans to put the DUT's vlan10 at 10.38.22.34/27.
It appeared exactly once in roughly 25 captures across four cases.


